    "['user privilege level [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set system login class SSH-class permissions all', 'parent_command': ['set system services ssh connection-limit [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set system services ssh connection-limit [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Juniper equivalent command for `user privilege level 3`:\\n\\n##\\nset system login class SSH-class permissions all\\nset system login user client001 class SSH-class\\nset system login user client002 class SSH-class\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Juniper, privilege levels are handled via login classes rather than direct privilege level assignments\\n2. We create a class \"SSH-class\" with \"all\" permissions (equivalent to privilege level 3 in Huawei)\\n3. Then assign this class to each SSH user\\n4. Note that this is a functional equivalent - Juniper\\'s permission model is more granular than Huawei\\'s privilege levels', 'trans_template': 'set system login class SSH-class permissions all'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set system login user client001 class SSH-class', 'parent_command': ['set system services ssh connection-limit [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set system services ssh connection-limit [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Juniper equivalent command for `user privilege level 3`:\\n\\n##\\nset system login class SSH-class permissions all\\nset system login user client001 class SSH-class\\nset system login user client002 class SSH-class\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Juniper, privilege levels are handled via login classes rather than direct privilege level assignments\\n2. We create a class \"SSH-class\" with \"all\" permissions (equivalent to privilege level 3 in Huawei)\\n3. Then assign this class to each SSH user\\n4. Note that this is a functional equivalent - Juniper\\'s permission model is more granular than Huawei\\'s privilege levels', 'trans_template': 'set system login user client001 class SSH-class'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set system login user client002 class SSH-class', 'parent_command': ['set system services ssh connection-limit [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set system services ssh connection-limit [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Juniper equivalent command for `user privilege level 3`:\\n\\n##\\nset system login class SSH-class permissions all\\nset system login user client001 class SSH-class\\nset system login user client002 class SSH-class\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Juniper, privilege levels are handled via login classes rather than direct privilege level assignments\\n2. We create a class \"SSH-class\" with \"all\" permissions (equivalent to privilege level 3 in Huawei)\\n3. Then assign this class to each SSH user\\n4. Note that this is a functional equivalent - Juniper\\'s permission model is more granular than Huawei\\'s privilege levels', 'trans_template': 'set system login user client002 class SSH-class'}]]": 1
